{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_lob77gvl5cs7noo7ojcfr7jpvmljc3z5uhkfip2667videjzgasa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. All four phases complete.\n\n## What I found\n\nThe translation compiled cleanly and passed every happy-path and error-path comparison. Two real mismatches surfaced only under adversarial input:\n\n**1. `strcpy` overruns past the end of a record array (wrong output *and* wrong exit status).**\n\n`parse_command` truncates tokens to 63 bytes, but `cmd_adduser` `strcpy`s them into 32-byte fields. Writing into `users[9].password` runs off the end of `users`, and in `.bss` the very next object is `user_count`:\n\n```\n0x4070a0  users        720   0x407370  user_count    0x407378  current_user\n0x407380  files      12240   0x40a350  file_count    0x40a360  variables ...\n```\n\nBecause the reference build is unoptimised, the next statements reload the clobbered counter. The result is exact and depends only on the password length — 40 bytes zeroes `user_count` (`Users: 1/10`), 41 makes it 80 (`Users: 81/10`), and ≥42 makes it wild so the following store faults. The C died of `SIGSEGV` with **0 bytes** of stdout; the Rust exited 0 with 4075 bytes. The same thing happens via `files[19].owner`, whose source is `current_user->name`.\n\nThe old code modelled each array as an isolated buffer with slack, with counters as plain Rust fields nothing could corrupt. I rewrote it to model the reference build's writable segment as one flat array at the real link addresses, with the counters living *inside* it and `current_user` held as a raw address so it can dangle. Leaving the mapped window raises a genuine `SIGSEGV` (restoring the default handler first, or Rust's stack-overflow handler would turn it into `SIGABRT`).\n\n**2. stdout flush points (only observable on a crash).**\n\nA `SIGSEGV` runs no cleanup, so glibc's buffer is discarded and only whole 4096-byte chunks reach the pipe. The old code used `BufWriter<Stdout>` and flushed after every `\"> \"` prompt, emitting bytes the C never did. `Out` now writes to fd 1 through its own buffer sized from `fstat(1).st_blksize`, line-buffered only when `isatty(1)` — glibc's own rule.\n\n## Verification\n\nI replayed the suite's full 281-input corpus through a `gcc --coverage` copy of the C to check coverage objectively rather than by eye. That flagged **7 branch outcomes no test reached**, all the same latent state: `current_user != NULL` while `logged_in == 0`. `cmd_logout` can never produce it (it NULLs the pointer too) — it's reachable *only* through the bug above, once `user_count` is 0 and `adduser` clears the flag on the record `current_user` still points at. The Rust already matched; it's now pinned by a test, bringing the C to **100% of lines, branches, and branch outcomes**.\n\nGuard rails so the crash tests can't pass vacuously: `crash_paths_are_really_crash_paths` asserts the *C* is killed by signal 11, and `harness_observes_real_output` asserts the harness sees real output.\n\nGate: both build warning-free; 39 tests pass in debug *and* release; none `#[ignore]`d; `.bss` addresses re-verified identical after a clean rebuild; `c_src/` checksums unchanged (only `build/` added, as instructed). Findings are written up in `translation/ERRORS.md`.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: matching the crash behaviour requires hard-coding the reference build's `.bss` addresses. That's exact for this toolchain (verified GCC 11.5.0, `-O0`, non-PIE) but a different compiler or optimisation level could reorder those globals and shift the thresholds. The addresses are documented in a module comment in `main.rs` so the assumption is visible if the build ever changes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1079,"cache_read_input_tokens":196617,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1079,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c55a84fa-1173-4eb1-9236-1341447b285e","uuid":"a0991627-d12f-45fe-a164-90b835a69ce8","timestamp":"2026-08-28T06:03:27.294Z"}
